var e=[{id:`fundamental-url-walkthrough`,pathId:`foundations-of-the-web`,order:0,title:`What Happens When You Type a URL`,subtitle:`The canonical interview question. The user types example.com and presses enter. What happens? — fifteen separate things, each its own subsystem. The depth at which you can describe`,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[],story:{headline:`The handshake tax.`,paragraphs:[`Two days before Black Friday, checkout p95 went from 400 ms to 4.1 seconds. The war room spun up. Database CPU: 30%. App servers: idle. Someone said 'scale the load balancer,' someone else said 'scale the database,' and both were wrong — because nothing on any dashboard was red.`,`I opened a fresh incognito browser — deliberately, to skip every warm cache — and watched the network waterfall instead of the dashboards. DNS: 8 ms, cached. TCP: 14 ms. TLS: 380 ms. Then it repeated — six times — because we were still on HTTP/1.1, and the browser opens six parallel connections, each paying the full TCP + TLS 1.2 handshake. Three round trips before a single byte of checkout HTML moved.`,`The fix wasn't a bigger fleet. It was flipping on TLS 1.3, session resumption, and HTTP/2 — config changes, one deploy. p95 fell back under 500 ms before the turkey went in the oven. The fifteen steps everyone memorizes for interviews? That night they were a debugging map, and the map pointed exactly where the dashboards couldn't.`],problem:`Checkout latency 10x'd under load while every server dashboard stayed green — the time was hiding in connection setup, not compute.`},tech:{stack:[`DNS Caching`,`TCP Handshake`,`TLS 1.3`,`0-RTT Resumption`,`HTTP/2 Multiplexing`,`QUIC`,`CDN Edge`,`HPACK`,`Brotli Compression`,`Critical Rendering Path`],note:`The request path is a chain of caches and handshakes: DNS, TCP, TLS, HTTP, and the browser's render pipeline. Every layer exists to shave round trips — or avoid them entirely.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the green dashboards lied`,body:[`Here's the thing nobody tells you about this interview question: it's not trivia, it's a causal chain. When someone types a URL, time gets spent in exactly four buckets — resolve, connect, transfer, render — and each bucket has a different owner, a different failure mode, and a different fix. Memorizing the fifteen steps is table stakes; using them as a debugging decision tree is the actual skill.`,`That night, the APM tool showed a healthy app, because the app was healthy — the 3.7 extra seconds were spent before the first packet ever reached it. 'Why not just look at traces?' Because most tracing starts at the load balancer. The devtools waterfall starts at the keystroke. When nothing is red, you need a model of where time goes — and this is that model.`],quote:`A dashboard tells you what's red. A mental model tells you where to look when nothing is.`,table:{headers:[`Phase`,`Typical budget`,`Owner`,`Common culprit`],rows:[[`Resolve`,`0–30 ms`,`DNS / recursive resolver`,`Low TTL, resolver outage`],[`Connect`,`30–100 ms`,`Edge / LB / TLS config`,`TLS 1.2, no resumption`],[`Transfer`,`100–500 ms`,`CDN / app / DB`,`Cache miss, N+1 queries`],[`Render`,`100–1000 ms`,`Frontend`,`Blocking JS/CSS, layout shift`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — What Happens When You Type a URL`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-url-walkthrough.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking Resolve: the lookup that almost never happens`,body:[`Step one surprises people: the DNS lookup almost never happens. Browser cache, OS stub cache, the recursive resolver's cache — by the time you need an answer, someone usually has it. A full uncached recursive walk — root, TLD, authoritative — is the exception, not the rule.`,`So the real engineering question isn't 'how does DNS work,' it's 'what TTL did you choose?' Short TTLs buy fast failover and cost you a resolver storm on every traffic spike. Long TTLs are cheap and cache-friendly but slow your failover to the TTL itself. And before any of this, HSTS may have rewritten http to https with zero network calls — a policy cache hiding in plain sight.`],quote:`The fastest DNS lookup is the one your TTL policy let you skip.`,table:{headers:[`TTL choice`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`60 s`,`Fast failover`,`Resolver storm on spikes`,`Active failover only`],[`300–3600 s`,`Cheap, cache-friendly`,`Slow failover`,`Stable front-door IPs`],[`0`,`Instant changes`,`Full recursive walk per user`,`Almost never`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — What Happens When You Type a URL`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-url-walkthrough.html`}},{heading:`03 · Walking Connect: paying the round-trip tax`,body:[`Connect is where the round-trip tax lives, and it's paid per connection. TCP: one RTT. TLS 1.2: two more. TLS 1.3: one — because the key share rides inside the ClientHello. Repeat visitors get 0-RTT resumption, where the first HTTP request literally attaches to the ClientHello itself.`,`Now multiply: on HTTP/1.1 the browser opens six parallel connections, and each one pays the full handshake. That was our 380 ms — six times over. HTTP/2 multiplexes many streams over one connection, and HPACK crushes the repeated headers. And QUIC? It merges transport and crypto into a single UDP handshake and kills TCP's head-of-line blocking — for roughly a third of modern traffic, the TCP and TLS steps are now one step.`],quote:`Every connection you open pays the handshake tax again — multiplexing is how you stop paying it six times.`,table:{headers:[`Protocol stack`,`RTT before first byte`,`Connections`,`Fit`],rows:[[`HTTP/1.1 + TLS 1.2`,`~3 (×6 connections)`,`6`,`Legacy only`],[`HTTP/2 + TLS 1.3`,`~2`,`1`,`Default today`],[`HTTP/3 (QUIC)`,`~1 (0 on resumption)`,`1 (UDP)`,`Lossy / mobile networks`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — What Happens When You Type a URL`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-url-walkthrough.html`}},{heading:`04 · Walking Transfer: physics sets the floor`,body:[`Transfer is where physics sets the floor. Light in fiber moves at about two-thirds c, so every 100 km of distance costs roughly a millisecond of round trip — no amount of bandwidth fixes that. The CDN exists to cheat distance: it moves the cache into the user's metro, so a hit returns at edge latency and never touches your origin.`,`But cacheability is decided by headers, not by wishful thinking. One stray Set-Cookie or a Vary: * on a static asset and the edge won't cache a byte — your 95% hit ratio becomes zero, and every user pays the full trip through the LB, the app, and the database. Brotli shrinks the payload; it doesn't shrink the distance. This is why 'add more origin servers' is so often the wrong lever.`],quote:`You can't bandwidth your way out of distance — you cache it, colocate it, or eat the RTT.`,table:{headers:[`Lever`,`What it fixes`,`What it can't fix`,`Real cost`],rows:[[`CDN edge cache`,`Distance to static assets`,`Dynamic, personalized bytes`,`Header discipline`],[`Brotli / gzip`,`Bytes on the wire`,`Round trips`,`CPU at the edge`],[`More origin servers`,`Compute saturation`,`Handshake + distance latency`,`Money — usually the wrong lever`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — What Happens When You Type a URL`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-url-walkthrough.html`}},{heading:`05 · Walking Render: the last 100 ms belong to the frontend`,body:[`The last stretch belongs to the frontend, and it's where well-run backends still feel slow. HTML becomes the DOM, CSS becomes the CSSOM, they merge into the render tree, then layout, then paint. Blocking CSS delays first paint; synchronous scripts in the head stall the parser while the page sits blank.`,`The fixes are boring and effective: defer or async your scripts, preload the critical CSS and fonts, size your images so layout doesn't jump. And note — server-side rendering doesn't remove this path, it just moves where the HTML gets built. The browser still has to parse, layout, and paint. TTFB is where the backend's job ends; the user judges you on first paint.`],quote:`TTFB is where the backend's job ends — the user judges you on first paint.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — What Happens When You Type a URL`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-url-walkthrough.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The full request path, with every cache decision point`,caption:`Four phases — resolve, connect, transfer, render — each with a shortcut that skips the expensive part.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  U["User types example.com"] --> P["URL parse + HSTS check"]
  P --> BC{"Browser cache fresh?"}
  BC -- "yes" --> R["Render from disk"]
  BC -- "no" --> DNS["DNS: browser → OS → recursive resolver"]
  DNS --> TCP["TCP handshake (1 RTT)"]
  TCP --> TLS["TLS 1.3 handshake (1 RTT)"]
  TLS --> EDGE{"CDN edge cache hit?"}
  EDGE -- "yes" --> R
  EDGE -- "no" --> LB["L7 load balancer"]
  LB --> APP["App server"]
  APP --> CACHE[("Cache")]
  APP --> DB[("Database")]
  APP --> RESP["Response: brotli + cache headers"]
  RESP --> R`},{id:`handshake-timeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Connection timeline: from keystroke to first byte`,caption:`TLS 1.3 folds the crypto handshake into one round trip — then HTTP/2 multiplexes every request over that single connection.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  participant B as Browser
  participant R as Recursive DNS
  participant E as CDN Edge
  participant O as Origin

  B->>R: where is example.com?
  R-->>B: 203.0.113.7 (cached, TTL 300s)
  B->>E: TCP SYN
  E-->>B: SYN-ACK
  B->>E: ACK + TLS ClientHello (key share, SNI)
  E-->>B: ServerHello + cert + Finished
  Note over B,E: TLS 1.3 done in 1 RTT — app data flows next
  B->>E: HTTP/2 GET / (HPACK-compressed headers)
  E->>O: forward on cache miss
  O-->>E: 200 HTML
  E-->>B: 200 OK (brotli, Cache-Control)`}],takeaways:[`Budget by round trips: HTTP/1.1 + TLS 1.2 costs ~3 RTT × 6 connections before first byte; TLS 1.3 + HTTP/2 cuts it to ~2 RTT on one connection.`,`Keep DNS TTLs ≥ 300 s for stable front doors — a 30 s TTL turns every traffic spike into a full recursive-walk storm.`,`One Set-Cookie or Vary: * header on a static asset can drop a CDN hit ratio from ~95% to 0% — audit response headers before you scale origin.`,`Light in fiber buys you roughly 200 km per millisecond of round trip — beyond that, only caching or colocation beats physics.`,`In a latency incident, read the network waterfall before the database dashboard: resolve → connect → transfer → render localizes the regression in one screen.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — What Happens When You Type a URL`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-url-walkthrough.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-dns`,pathId:`foundations-of-the-web`,order:1,title:`DNS`,subtitle:`DNS is a global hierarchy of caches in front of a tiny authoritative truth. A lookup almost never reaches the authoritative servers; it terminates at one of millions of recursive c`,minutes:11,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`Recursive resolvers`,`TTLs`,`authoritative`],story:{headline:`We changed the DNS record at 11 PM. The internet disagreed until the next night.`,paragraphs:[`Friday, 11:04 PM. We cut api.acme.io over to a new provider — one A record change at the authoritative zone, applied in seconds. Dashboards went green, error rates flat, and someone opened the pizza box. Textbook migration. We went to bed feeling clever.`,`Saturday, 9 AM: support tickets. A solid chunk of users getting connection refused. Traces showed their traffic still hammering the old provider's IPs — which we had decommissioned at midnight. An hour of digging found the culprit: the A record carried a TTL of 86400, set fourteen months earlier by someone 'optimizing DNS load.' Every recursive resolver on Earth that had answered that name on Friday was now faithfully serving a corpse for 24 hours.`,`The fix was humiliating and instructive: we resurrected the old stack as a dumb reverse proxy to the new one and fed the stragglers for a day. The permanent rule came out of the postmortem: TTL 60 on anything that can fail over, and lower the TTL a full day before any planned cutover. 'DNS propagation' was never some mysterious mechanism — it was our own 86400 running out in public.`],problem:`How do you update an answer the entire internet has cached, when millions of independent resolvers each keep your old answer for exactly as long as your old TTL says — and you can't reach a single one of them?`},tech:{stack:[`Recursive Resolver`,`Authoritative NS`,`TTL`,`A / CNAME`,`Negative Caching`,`Health-Checked DNS`],note:`Your OS stub asks a recursive resolver, which caches every answer for the record's TTL and only walks the root → TLD → authoritative chain on a miss. TTLs, record types, and negative caching are the knobs that decide how fast the world believes your changes.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why is DNS fast at all?`,body:[`Here's where I'd start: DNS answers billions of lookups a day — why doesn't it fall over? The answer is almost disappointing. It's fast because it's lazy. Trace any real lookup and you'll find it terminates at a cache — your OS, your browser, your ISP's resolver — long before it gets anywhere near the servers that actually own the record. The authoritative infrastructure is tiny precisely because it is rarely asked.`,`Why not skip the caches and query authoritative directly? Do the math: every page load fires a handful of lookups, and a direct walk costs three extra round trips per name — a query storm no root infrastructure absorbs. Caching moves 95%+ of that load to the edge and cuts latency from hundreds of milliseconds to single digits. The price you pay is staleness, and staleness has a name and a number: the TTL.`,`So when someone tells me 'DNS is slow,' my first question is: slow where? A cache hit is 1–10 ms. A cold walk is 3+ round trips. Almost every 'slow DNS' incident I've debugged turned out to be a cold resolver, a negatively-cached failure, or one dead resolver in the OS's list timing out.`],quote:`DNS is fast because it's lazy — a tiny authoritative truth in front of an enormous, TTL-bounded cache.`,table:{headers:[`Lookup path`,`Latency`,`Authoritative load`,`Fit`],rows:[[`OS stub cache`,`~0 ms`,`none`,`repeat lookups on the same host`],[`Recursive cache hit`,`1–10 ms`,`none`,`the 95% case for all traffic`],[`Full cold walk (root→TLD→auth)`,`3+ RTTs, 50–300 ms`,`1 query per level`,`first contact or post-TTL refresh`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — DNS`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-dns.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: delegation, not ownership`,body:[`Next thought: how does a system with no central owner stay coherent? Delegation. The root doesn't know example.com's address — it knows who runs .com. The .com servers don't know www's address — they know example.com's nameservers. Each layer only knows enough to point one level down. Thirteen root server identities (anycast, so thousands of physical machines), roughly 1500 TLDs, hundreds of millions of domains below that.`,`Why not one flat global table? Because updates, trust, and load all scale badly when flat. The hierarchy means a domain owner changes their zone without asking anyone's permission — the delegation chain simply routes to whatever they published. In practice you'll live at the bottom of the chain: A/AAAA for addresses, CNAME for aliases, MX for mail, TXT for SPF/DKIM/verification, NS for delegation itself. Ninety percent of your career in DNS is A and CNAME.`,`One gotcha worth saying out loud: CNAME is not allowed at a zone apex — example.com can't be a CNAME because SOA and NS records must coexist at that exact name. Managed providers fake it with ALIAS/ANAME flattening at their edge, which is a vendor feature, not a DNS feature.`],quote:`Nobody owns DNS; many somebodies each own a tiny piece, and each piece only knows where the next one starts.`,table:{headers:[`Record`,`Answers`,`Gotcha`],rows:[[`A / AAAA`,`hostname → IPv4 / IPv6`,`the workhorse; TTL discipline lives here`],[`CNAME`,`alias → another hostname`,`illegal at zone apex; use ALIAS/ANAME there`],[`MX`,`mail exchanger for the domain`,`must point to a name, not an IP`],[`TXT`,`arbitrary text`,`SPF, DKIM, domain-verification challenges`],[`NS`,`which servers are authoritative`,`where delegation actually lives`],[`SRV`,`service discovery`,`non-HTTP protocols; you'll rarely touch it`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — DNS`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-dns.html`}},{heading:`03 · TTLs: the most important number nobody on your team knows`,body:[`Now the number the war story turned on. Every record carries a TTL: how long any downstream cache may keep it before re-asking. There is no push mechanism, no 'propagation protocol' — the change is instant at the authoritative servers. What people call propagation is simply every recursive resolver on the planet aging out your old answer at the pace your old TTL set. Old TTL 86400? The world converges in 24 hours. That's the entire mechanism.`,`So the play is proactive, not reactive: 24 hours before a planned cutover, drop the TTL to 60. Every cache that refreshes during that window now holds a short-TTL version, and the actual change propagates in about a minute. I have watched teams skip this step and spend an entire weekend paying for it, exactly like we did.`,`Why not just run TTL 60 everywhere, forever? Because you're trading cache hit rate for agility — every resolver re-asks every minute, your authoritative servers and your DNS bill feel it, and p99 latency creeps up. TTL is a dial between 'fast answers' and 'fast changes.' Set it per record, deliberately, and write it down.`],quote:`'DNS propagation' isn't a mechanism — it's your old TTL running out in public.`,table:{headers:[`Record kind`,`Suggested TTL`,`Why`],rows:[[`Failover / load-balancer IP`,`60 s`,`bounds how long users hit a dead IP`],[`Standard app endpoint`,`300 s`,`5 minutes of staleness is fine`],[`Vanity CNAME, never changes`,`86400 s`,`maximum cache benefit, zero agility needed`],[`Pre-cutover window`,`60 s for 1× old TTL`,`caches refill with short-TTL copies before you switch`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — DNS`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-dns.html`}},{heading:`04 · Negative caching and health-aware DNS`,body:[`Two wrinkles that bite people every week. First: resolvers cache failure too. Ask for a name that doesn't exist and the resolver stores the NXDOMAIN — typically for about 5 minutes, governed by the SOA record's negative TTL. Fix the typo at the authoritative servers and users still see 'server not found' for minutes afterward, because the resolver remembers your mistake as faithfully as your answers.`,`Second: health-aware DNS. Route 53, Cloudflare, NS1 will tie a record to a probe — if the IP behind your A record fails its health check, the provider hands out a different one. It's the cheapest cross-region failover that works for any TCP-based service, not just HTTP. But look at the bound: detection is the probe interval, switchover is the TTL. A 60-second TTL with 30-second probes means up to ~90 seconds of users knocking on a dead door.`,`Why not rely on it alone? For user-facing HTTP I'll pair it with low TTLs and client-side retry logic, or hide the whole problem behind a load balancer so DNS only fails over between entire regions. DNS failover is a blunt, slow instrument — brilliant for region-scale failures, useless for per-server blips.`],quote:`Resolvers cache your failures as faithfully as your answers — plan for both.`,table:{headers:[`Failover mechanism`,`Detection`,`Worst-case switch`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Health-checked DNS`,`provider probe`,`TTL + probe interval (~90 s)`,`cross-region failover for any TCP service`],[`Client retry to backup IP`,`connection error`,`next request`,`apps whose code you control`],[`Anycast`,`BGP re-route`,`seconds–minutes`,`global edge services, not app-level`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — DNS`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-dns.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`dns-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The shape of DNS: enormous lazy edge, tiny authoritative truth`,caption:`A lookup terminates at the first cache that has a fresh answer. The root → TLD → authoritative chain is only walked on a miss — which is the rare case.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Client["Client side"]
        STUB["Stub resolver<br/>in your OS"]
    end
    subgraph Edge["The enormous lazy layer"]
        REC["Recursive resolver<br/>ISP / 8.8.8.8 / 1.1.1.1"]
        CACHE[("Cache<br/>every answer lives TTL seconds")]
        REC --- CACHE
    end
    subgraph Truth["The tiny authoritative truth"]
        ROOT["Root servers<br/>13 identities, anycast"]
        TLD["TLD servers<br/>.com + about 1500 siblings"]
        AUTH["Authoritative NS<br/>owns example.com"]
        ROOT --> TLD --> AUTH
    end
    STUB -->|"1 ask"| REC
    REC -.->|"2 on miss only"| ROOT
    AUTH -.->|"3 answer + TTL"| REC
    REC -->|"4 cached answer"| STUB`},{id:`dns-resolution-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One lookup, two worlds: cache hit vs. the cold walk`,caption:`The hit path is one round trip and single-digit milliseconds. The miss path costs three delegations before anyone who actually knows the answer is asked — and then the answer is cached for TTL seconds.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant App as Browser
    participant Stub as Stub resolver
    participant Rec as Recursive resolver
    participant Root as Root server
    participant TLD as .com TLD
    participant Auth as example.com NS

    App->>Stub: resolve api.example.com
    Stub->>Rec: A api.example.com?
    alt cache hit and TTL fresh
        Rec-->>Stub: cached A record (1-10 ms)
    else cache miss
        Rec->>Root: who runs .com?
        Root-->>Rec: NS list for .com
        Rec->>TLD: who runs example.com?
        TLD-->>Rec: NS list for example.com
        Rec->>Auth: A api.example.com?
        Auth-->>Rec: 203.0.113.7, TTL 60
        Rec-->>Stub: 203.0.113.7 (now cached)
    end
    Stub-->>App: 203.0.113.7`}],takeaways:[`Set TTL by change-frequency: 60 s for failover-bound records, 300 s for normal endpoints, 86400 s only for records you genuinely never touch.`,`Before any planned cutover, drop the TTL to 60 s at least one old-TTL period ahead (24 h TTL → lower it 24 h early); the change then converges in ~1 minute.`,`Budget health-checked DNS failover as TTL + probe interval: 60 s TTL with 30 s probes means up to ~90 s of traffic to a dead IP.`,`A fixed NXDOMAIN keeps failing for ~5 min (SOA negative TTL) — don't retest or page faster than that after fixing a typo.`,`Cache hit ≈ 1–10 ms vs. cold walk ≈ 3 round trips / 50–300 ms — that ~30× gap is why TTL, not raw speed, is DNS's most important number.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — DNS`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-dns.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-https`,pathId:`foundations-of-the-web`,order:2,title:`HTTPS`,subtitle:`HTTPS is HTTP inside a TLS tunnel. The browser, the server, and a chain of certificate authorities cooperate so that nobody between them can read or modify the traffic. The handsha`,minutes:12,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`ECDHE`,`Let's Encrypt`,`TLS 1.3`],story:{headline:`2 a.m. Sunday: the padlock turned red, and the renewal had been dead for a month.`,paragraphs:[`The pager goes off at 2:07 on a Sunday. Checkout conversion hasn't degraded — it has fallen off a cliff. Support is drowning in screenshots of a full-screen red warning: 'Your connection is not private.' App logs are spotless, because nothing in the app is wrong. The TLS certificate expired at midnight.`,`The twist is what makes it a war story. The renewal job had been failing for 31 days — a newly deployed WAF rule started blocking requests to /.well-known/acme-challenge, the ACME HTTP-01 challenge died quietly, the cron logged a failure nobody read, and the fallback was silence. Thirty days of quiet decay, then a hard cliff the moment the cert expired. Browsers don't negotiate around an expired certificate; they refuse, loudly, in front of every customer.`,`The fix itself took ten minutes: whitelist the challenge path, re-run the renewal, done in seconds — that part is Let's Encrypt's gift to the world. The week that followed was the real lesson: expiry monitoring at T-21 days, a watch on Certificate Transparency logs for any cert issued against our domains, and a monthly staging rehearsal of the renewal path. The padlock had been lying to us by omission. It only speaks when it breaks.`],problem:`Automated certificate renewal that fails silently for a month, then expires at the worst possible hour — with zero telemetry watching the one thing browsers refuse to negotiate around.`},tech:{stack:[`TLS 1.3`,`ECDHE`,`ACME`,`Let's Encrypt`,`Certificate Transparency`,`HSTS`],note:`The moving parts between a browser's ClientHello and a padlock that actually means something: ephemeral key exchange, automated issuance, public accountability for mis-issuance, and the policy header that forces it all.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'just encrypt it' doesn't work`,body:[`Start where every attacker lives: the network between two people who trust each other. A coffee-shop router, your ISP, a corporate inspection box, a national firewall — every one of them can read plain HTTP byte-for-byte, and the documented cases of ad injection and credential capture aren't hypotheticals, they're history. So the obvious instinct is 'just encrypt it.' Here's why that instinct alone collapses.`,`Encryption without authentication is a trap. If the server's identity isn't proven, a man-in-the-middle simply terminates your encrypted session and opens a second one to the real server — congratulations, you've built a private tunnel to an attacker. Integrity is the same story: ciphertext that can be bit-flipped in transit is a silent corruption bug. And authenticity without the other two is a signed letter read aloud in a crowded room.`,`So HTTPS is three properties that only work as a set: confidentiality — eavesdroppers see ciphertext; integrity — tampering is detected, not accepted; authenticity — the server proves it owns the domain. Drop any one and the other two are nearly worthless. That's the mental model I want you carrying into every TLS config you'll ever touch.`],quote:`The padlock isn't a feature. It's the absence of a long list of attacks.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Plain HTTP`,`Zero setup, trivially debuggable`,`Anyone on path reads and rewrites traffic`,`Never — localhost only`],[`Encryption only (self-signed)`,`Wire is opaque`,`MITM terminates both halves; trains users to click through warnings`,`Internal tools with pinned certs`],[`Full TLS (conf + int + auth)`,`Eavesdropping, tampering, impersonation all blocked`,`Cert ops, handshake cost, config surface`,`Everything user-facing and most service-to-service`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — HTTPS`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-https.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the handshake: how three round trips became one`,body:[`Now the handshake — and the genuinely clever bit. In TLS 1.3 the ClientHello doesn't just list cipher suites; it carries an ECDHE key share up front, speculatively, for the most likely curves. The client is placing a bet before the server has even spoken. When the bet lands — and it almost always does — the server picks that curve and returns its own key share, the certificate, and its Finished message in the same flight. One round trip, where 2010-era setups stacked up to three.`,`The exchange itself is ephemeral Diffie–Hellman on elliptic curves: both sides generate throwaway key pairs, swap public halves, and compute the same session secret without the secret ever crossing the wire. When the connection dies, the keys die with it. That's forward secrecy — record everything today, steal the server's private key next year, and the captured traffic stays ciphertext. This is also why TLS 1.3 deleted static RSA key exchange outright: no forward secrecy, no seat at the table.`,`Then there's 0-RTT for returning clients: a pre-shared key from the previous session lets the browser bundle the HTTP request with the ClientHello, so the response lands in a single round trip total. The catch is replay — an attacker who captures that 0-RTT flight can replay it, and the server can't tell the copy from the original. So the rule is simple: 0-RTT for replay-safe GETs of public resources; anything with side effects rides the standard 1-RTT path.`],quote:`The client bets on a curve before the server speaks. When the bet lands, you've bought back a round trip.`,table:{headers:[`Handshake mode`,`Round trips`,`Pros`,`Cons / Fit`],rows:[[`TLS 1.2 full (RSA key exchange)`,`2`,`Legacy compatibility`,`No forward secrecy — removed in TLS 1.3`],[`TLS 1.3 full (ECDHE)`,`1`,`Forward secrecy, fresh keys per connection`,`Default for every new connection`],[`TLS 1.3 0-RTT (PSK resumption)`,`0 — data in first flight`,`Fastest path for returning clients`,`Replayable — safe GETs only, never POSTs`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — HTTPS`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-https.html`}},{heading:`03 · Who vouches for the server? The trust chain`,body:[`The server sends a certificate — but why does your browser believe a word of it? Because the browser ships with a root store: a list of certificate authorities it trusts unconditionally. The server presents its leaf cert plus intermediates, and the browser walks the chain, validating each signature up to a root it already trusts. That's the entire trick — trust delegated down a chain, where every link has to hold.`,`For most of the web's life this was expensive and manual: fifty to five hundred dollars a year, application forms, renewals via emailed PDFs. Let's Encrypt blew that up — the ACME protocol lets a server prove domain control through an HTTP or DNS challenge and receive a 90-day certificate in seconds, for free. And here's the part engineers miss: 90 days is not an inconvenience, it's the feature. Short lifetimes force automated renewal, which forces correct configuration, which quietly makes the whole ecosystem safer.`,`The last pillar is Certificate Transparency: every publicly trusted cert is written to append-only, public logs, and browsers refuse certs without proof of logging. If a CA gets tricked into issuing your domain to an attacker, it's no longer a silent failure — it's a public event you can detect and act on. Which is exactly why our war story hurt the way it did: the weakest link in the trust chain wasn't the cryptography. It was a WAF rule nobody was watching.`],quote:`A 90-day certificate isn't a burden. It's a forcing function for the automation you should have built anyway.`,table:{headers:[`Issuance model`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Commercial CA, manual`,`Long lifetimes, EV options, vendor support`,`Cost, manual renewals, slow rotation during incidents`,`Enterprises with existing PKI workflows`],[`ACME / Let's Encrypt`,`Free, issued in seconds, forces automation`,`90-day renewals demand working automation and monitoring`,`Default for public web and APIs`],[`Self-signed / private PKI`,`No external dependency, full control`,`Distribution problem; browsers warn unless pinned`,`Internal service meshes, mTLS fleets`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — HTTPS`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-https.html`}},{heading:`04 · What the padlock does not promise`,body:[`Let's read the padlock's fine print, because this is where security audits go sideways. First: HTTPS authenticates the server, full stop. The client is anonymous unless you configure mTLS — so if your threat model includes a rogue service calling your internal API, the padlock has nothing to say about it.`,`Second: the connection is private, but the fact that you're connecting is not. SNI — the hostname inside the ClientHello — crosses the wire in the clear, so an on-path observer learns which sites you visit even if not which pages. Encrypted Client Hello is shipping and closes that hole, but adoption is uneven. Third, and most important: TLS protects the pipe, not the endpoints. A compromised server reads plaintext all day long, because encryption happens after the application already has the data.`,`And one for the product folks: the padlock means 'this connection is secure to this domain,' not 'this domain is honest.' Phishing sites carry valid certificates like everyone else. HSTS solves a different problem — forcing HTTPS and killing downgrade attacks — with preload as the nuclear option: once you're on that list, removal takes months, so test every subdomain before you submit.`],quote:`TLS guards the pipe. It has no opinion about what's waiting at either end.`,table:{headers:[`Threat`,`Covered by HTTPS?`,`What closes the gap`],rows:[[`Passive eavesdropping on path`,`Yes — ciphertext on the wire`,`—`],[`Tampering / injection in transit`,`Yes — integrity checks fail closed`,`—`],[`Server impersonation`,`Yes — chain to a trusted root`,`CT logs to catch mis-issuance`],[`Client impersonation`,`No`,`mTLS or token auth at the app layer`],[`Hostname visibility (SNI)`,`No`,`Encrypted Client Hello (rolling out)`],[`Compromised server`,`No`,`Endpoint hardening, least privilege, secrets hygiene`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — HTTPS`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-https.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`tls13-handshake`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`TLS 1.3 handshake — one round trip to encrypted`,caption:`The speculative key share in the ClientHello is what compresses three round trips into one; 0-RTT returning clients push data into the very first flight.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant B as Browser
    participant S as Edge Server
    B->>S: ClientHello — TLS 1.3, cipher list, SNI, ECDHE key share
    S-->>B: ServerHello + server key share, certificate, CertificateVerify, Finished
    Note over B,S: Both sides derive the same session secret via ECDHE — the secret never crosses the wire
    B->>S: Finished + first encrypted HTTP request
    S-->>B: Encrypted HTTP response
    Note over B,S: Total: 1 round trip. Returning clients may use 0-RTT and send data with the ClientHello — replay-safe GETs only`},{id:`acme-trust-chain`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`ACME issuance and the browser's chain walk`,caption:`From domain-control challenge to padlock: issuance, public CT logging, and the chain validation a browser performs on every handshake.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Server orders certificate via ACME"] --> B{"Challenge type?"}
    B -->|HTTP-01| C["Serve token at /.well-known/acme-challenge"]
    B -->|DNS-01| D["Publish TXT record on _acme-challenge"]
    C --> E["Let's Encrypt validates domain control"]
    D --> E
    E --> F["90-day certificate issued"]
    F --> G["Logged to public CT logs"]
    F --> H["Served during TLS handshake"]
    H --> I["Browser walks chain: leaf to intermediate to root"]
    I --> J{"Root trusted and cert in CT log?"}
    J -->|Yes| K["Padlock shown — handshake completes"]
    J -->|No| L["Full-screen warning page"]
    G -.->|mis-issuance becomes detectable| M["Domain owner monitors CT logs"]`}],takeaways:[`Terminate TLS at the edge (ALB / CloudFront / Front Door) and let ACME renew automatically — but rehearse the renewal path monthly; a 90-day cert gives you ~30 chances a year to catch a broken challenge before it becomes an outage.`,`Alert on certificate expiry at T-21 days, not T-0 — and watch Certificate Transparency logs for certs issued against your domains that you didn't request; mis-issuance is only detectable if someone is looking.`,`TLS 1.3 full handshake is 1 RTT (down from 2–3 a decade ago); enable 0-RTT only for replay-safe GETs of public resources — POSTs and anything with side effects must ride the standard 1-RTT path.`,`HSTS: max-age ≥ 31536000 (1 year) with includeSubDomains; only submit to preload after every subdomain serves HTTPS — preload removal takes months, so treat it as irreversible.`,`The padlock authenticates the server only — for client identity you need mTLS. And keep ECDHE-only suites: forward secrecy means a leaked private key next year can't decrypt the traffic you recorded today.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — HTTPS`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-https.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-http-headers`,pathId:`foundations-of-the-web`,order:3,title:`HTTP Headers`,subtitle:`There are hundreds of HTTP headers. Most you'll never see. The two dozen that matter — caching, security, content type, conditional requests, rate limits — are worth learning. The `,minutes:8,difficulty:3,tags:[`Rate limiter`],story:{headline:`The 3 AM invoice that belonged to someone else`,paragraphs:[`12:05 during a sudden traffic spike. Pager fires. Support is drowning in one ticket shape: "I logged in and saw another customer's invoice." Not a rendering bug, not a bad migration — the dashboard, behind the shiny new CDN shipped that afternoon, was serving User A's authenticated response to User B. Same URL, wrong human.`,`The war room traced it in an hour. The app set Cache-Control: max-age=300 on everything, because the team believed "no header means don't cache." The spec says the opposite: with no explicit policy, shared caches may heuristically cache. Worse, the CDN was configured cookie-blind on cache keys, so every authenticated GET looked identical to the edge. The edge cached one customer's invoice and replayed it to whoever asked next — five minutes at a time.`,`The fix wasn't ripping out the CDN. It was a per-route header contract: private, no-store on anything personalized; public with s-maxage on shared assets; Vary declared wherever a response depended on a request header; ETag revalidation so 304s could do the heavy lifting. Headers are the only API you have with every cache sitting between you and your user — and that night, ours was silent, which the protocol reads as "guess."`],problem:`How do you control every browser, proxy, and CDN between you and the user — without leaking personalized data or melting the origin?`},tech:{stack:[`Cache-Control`,`ETag / 304`,`Vary`,`CDN Edge Cache`,`Set-Cookie Flags`,`CORS Preflight`,`HPACK / QPACK`],note:`The header families and cache machinery the story meets, in order: response cache directives, validators, the edge cache that misbehaved, and the compression that makes header-heavy design cheap.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why did the edge cache everyone's invoice?`,body:[`Here's the mental model I want you to leave with: HTTP is a cache-coordination protocol wearing a transfer protocol's clothes. Every response you emit is a negotiation with caches you don't own — the browser, corporate proxies, your CDN. When you send no Cache-Control, you're not saying "don't cache me." You're saying "do whatever you think is best," and what caches think is best is heuristic caching — often something like 10% of the age since Last-Modified.`,`So the fix is never "turn off the CDN." It's a per-route policy: private, no-store for anything personalized; public with s-maxage for shared assets so the edge absorbs load; stale-while-revalidate so revalidation happens behind a stale copy instead of in front of a waiting user. And Vary — the header that tells a cache "your key must include these request headers too." Skip it and you either fragment the cache into uselessness or, on cookie-blind CDNs, you share one entry across every user.`],quote:`An absent Cache-Control isn't "don't cache" — it's "the cache is allowed to guess," and caches guess wrong at 3 AM.`,table:{headers:[`Directive`,`Who obeys it`,`What it buys`,`Failure mode when misused`],rows:[[`no-store`,`Every cache`,`Guaranteed non-caching for private data`,`Overused → origin melts, latency spikes`],[`no-cache`,`Every cache`,`Caching allowed, but must revalidate (304s)`,`Confused with no-store; kills hit rates`],[`max-age`,`Browsers + shared caches`,`Freshness window with zero origin hits`,`Too long on personalized routes → leaks`],[`s-maxage`,`CDNs/proxies only`,`Longer edge TTL than browser TTL`,`Ignored by browsers; surprises teams`],[`stale-while-revalidate`,`Browsers + CDNs`,`Serve stale while refreshing in background`,`Window too long → confusing UX`],[`immutable`,`Browsers`,`Skip revalidation on versioned assets`,`On unversioned URLs → permanent staleness`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — HTTP Headers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-http-headers.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what never belongs in the body`,body:[`Next thought: once you've decided what's cacheable, the second lever is not sending bytes at all. ETag is an opaque version tag; the client sends it back as If-None-Match, and if it still matches you return 304 Not Modified — a response with headers and no body. That's the cheapest successful response in HTTP. Last-Modified does the same dance with timestamps, but it's weaker: one-second granularity, clock skew, no help for content that changes multiple times a second.`,`Then there's the content negotiation family. Content-Type and Content-Encoding describe the body; Accept and Accept-Encoding let the client say what it can read. Get these wrong and you get the classic failures: gzip bytes with no Content-Encoding, so the browser renders garbage; or a missing Content-Type, so the browser sniffs and you've invented an XSS vector. If you don't know the length up front, Transfer-Encoding: chunked streams without one — never fake a Content-Length.`],quote:`A 304 is the cheapest byte you'll ever ship: all header, no body.`,table:{headers:[`Validator`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Strong ETag`,`Exact content fingerprint; byte-precise 304s`,`Costs hashing/serialization to compute`,`Default for JSON/API resources`],[`Weak ETag (W/)`,`Cheap; same-document semantics`,`Not byte-identical; range requests unusable`,`HTML pages, semantically-equal responses`],[`Last-Modified`,`Free from filesystem mtime`,`1-second resolution; clock skew`,`Static files, images`],[`ETag + long max-age`,`304s only when revalidation happens`,`Needs disciplined cache-busting URLs`,`Versioned static assets`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — HTTP Headers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-http-headers.html`}},{heading:`03 · Auth is a header problem — and cookies come with a bodyguard`,body:[`Third thought: authentication in HTTP is entirely a header conversation. Authorization: Bearer carries the token; WWW-Authenticate is the server saying "you need auth, and here's how." For APIs, mobile apps, and CLIs, bearer headers are the right shape — stateless, no ambient authority, explicit on every request.`,`But browsers are different, and this is where Set-Cookie earns its flags. HttpOnly keeps the token out of JavaScript's hands — the difference between an XSS bug and a session-theft bug. Secure forces HTTPS. SameSite=Lax blunts most CSRF. A JWT in localStorage has none of that armor: same header concept, zero bodyguard. So — cookies for browser sessions, bearer headers for everything else, and let WWW-Authenticate do the negotiating.`],quote:`A token in localStorage is an auth header without a bodyguard.`,table:{headers:[`Scheme`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Authorization: Bearer`,`Stateless; explicit; works everywhere`,`JS-readable; needs storage discipline`,`APIs, mobile, CLI, service-to-service`],[`Cookie + Set-Cookie flags`,`HttpOnly/SameSite armor; sent automatically`,`CSRF surface; credentials/CORS friction`,`Browser sessions`],[`Authorization: Basic`,`Trivial to implement`,`Credentials on every request; no expiry`,`Internal tools behind TLS only`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — HTTP Headers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-http-headers.html`}},{heading:`04 · The bouncer at the door: CORS and the security headers`,body:[`Fourth thought: CORS confuses everyone because it's inverted. It's not your server's wall — it's the browser's rule for whether to hand your response to JavaScript from another origin. The browser sends Origin; you answer with Access-Control-Allow-Origin. For anything non-simple, the browser first fires a preflight OPTIONS and won't send the real request until you answer. And the classic footgun: wildcards and credentials are mutually exclusive — Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * cannot ship alongside credentials.`,`Security headers are the same philosophy: one line each, whole attack classes blunted. Content-Security-Policy turns XSS from "game over" into "blocked by policy." Strict-Transport-Security pins the browser to HTTPS so a downgrade link can't bite. X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff disables MIME sniffing — the exact failure you invite by botching Content-Type. None of these are clever; all of them are load-bearing.`],quote:`CORS isn't a wall around your server — it's the browser deciding who may read the response.`,table:{headers:[`Header`,`Attack it stops`,`Cost when wrong`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Content-Security-Policy`,`XSS, injection`,`Over-strict policy breaks the app`,`Every HTML response`],[`Strict-Transport-Security`,`SSL downgrade, cookie theft`,`Locks the domain into HTTPS (good, but sticky)`,`Any HTTPS origin`],[`X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`,`MIME-sniffing exploits`,`Mis-typed Content-Type gets blocked (that's the point)`,`All responses`],[`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`,`Cross-origin data theft`,`* + credentials = browser rejects; too loose = leak`,`Every cross-origin API`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — HTTP Headers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-http-headers.html`}},{heading:`05 · "Headers are expensive" is a lie your HTTP/1.1 brain tells you`,body:[`Last thought, and it retires an old excuse. In HTTP/1.1, every request re-sent every header as plain text, so "headers are heavy" was fair. HTTP/2's HPACK and HTTP/3's QPACK compress them with static and dynamic tables — after the first occurrence, a repeated header like Authorization or User-Agent costs a handful of bytes on the wire.`,`Which means the modern move is to design with headers, not around them. Rate-limit state belongs in RateLimit-Remaining and Retry-After, not in a JSON body every client must parse. Trace context belongs in traceparent. Deprecation warnings belong in headers where proxies and middleware can see them. The body is for the payload; the headers are the control plane. That's the whole lesson: the two dozen headers you'll actually use are how you do mature API design without overloading the body.`],quote:`In HTTP/2+, a repeated header costs bytes, not round trips — so put your metadata in the control plane.`,table:{headers:[`Protocol`,`Header handling`,`Cost of repeated headers`,`Fit`],rows:[[`HTTP/1.1`,`Plain text, re-sent every request`,`Full bytes each time; keep them lean`,`Legacy clients, simple proxies`],[`HTTP/2 (HPACK)`,`Static + dynamic index tables`,`A few bytes after first send`,`Default for browsers + APIs`],[`HTTP/3 (QPACK)`,`HPACK adapted to QUIC streams`,`A few bytes; no head-of-line blocking`,`Lossy/mobile networks, QUIC frontends`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — HTTP Headers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-http-headers.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`cache-contract`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The cache contract: where the invoice leak happened`,caption:`One missing directive plus a cookie-blind cache key turned a shared edge into a data leak; the fix is a per-route header policy.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
  Req["GET /invoices/42<br/>Authorization: Bearer …"] --> Edge["CDN edge cache"]
  Edge -->|"cookie-blind config"| Key{"Cache key includes<br/>Vary headers?"}
  Key -->|"No — every user looks identical"| Hit["HIT: cached body<br/>belongs to another user"]
  Hit --> Leak["User B sees User A's invoice<br/>for the full max-age"]
  Key -->|"Yes — key fragments per user"| Miss["MISS: forward to origin"]
  Miss --> Origin["Origin server"]
  Origin -->|"personalized route"| Priv["Cache-Control: private, no-store"]
  Origin -->|"shared assets"| Pub["Cache-Control: public, s-maxage=86400,<br/>stale-while-revalidate=60"]
  Priv --> Edge
  Pub --> Store["Stored with correct Vary"] --> Client["Right body, right user"]`},{id:`conditional-304`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Conditional requests: the 304 fast path`,caption:`ETag + If-None-Match turns repeat reads into header-only responses; the CDN usually answers before the origin even wakes up.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant B as Browser
  participant E as CDN edge
  participant O as Origin
  B->>E: GET /app.js — If-None-Match: v7
  Note over E: Lookup keyed on URL + Vary: Accept-Encoding
  alt fresh copy in cache
    E-->>B: 304 Not Modified — zero body bytes
  else miss or revalidating
    E->>O: GET /app.js — If-None-Match: v7
    alt asset unchanged
      O-->>E: 304 Not Modified
      E-->>B: 304 + Age + Cache-Control
    else asset changed
      O-->>E: 200 + new ETag v8
      E-->>B: 200 + Cache-Control: max-age=31536000, immutable
    end
  end`}],takeaways:[`Memorize the ~2 dozen headers across six categories — caching, content, auth, CORS, security, metadata; the other ~200 are lookup vocabulary.`,`Default every authenticated route to Cache-Control: private, no-store and opt into public caching per route; one missing directive can serve User A's data to User B for the whole TTL.`,`Ship ETag + If-None-Match on cacheable reads: a 304 is header-only (~0 body bytes), and a well-chosen max-age means most repeat requests never reach origin at all.`,`Set s-maxage 10–100x your browser max-age so the CDN absorbs origin load, and add stale-while-revalidate=60 so revalidation hides behind a stale copy instead of a spinner.`,`Treat Vary as a security control, not a perf knob: any cacheable response that depends on a request header (Accept-Encoding, Authorization) must declare it — a missing Vary is a data-leak bug.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — HTTP Headers`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-http-headers.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-nginx`,pathId:`foundations-of-the-web`,order:4,title:`NGINX — 1M concurrent connections`,subtitle:`Apache forked a thread per connection and died at ~10k. NGINX uses an event loop per worker and lives at 1M. The architectural difference is one of the most consequential in web in`,minutes:10,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`Event loop`,`TLS`,`epoll`,`workers`],story:{headline:`MaxClients: 4,096. Queue: 40,000. The boxes ran out of threads before they ran out of CPU.`,paragraphs:[`Black Friday preview, 22:40. Our catalog edge ran Apache with the worker MPM on eight beefy boxes. The push notification went out, and within four minutes every node slammed into MaxClients. Load averages north of 200, swap in full thrash, SSH sessions dying faster than we could type into them. The kicker: the boxes were 15% CPU-utilized. We weren't out of compute. We were out of threads.`,`The whiteboard math at 3 AM was brutal. Worker MPM: roughly 1 MB of stack per connection-thread, plus a kernel scheduler juggling tens of thousands of mostly-sleeping threads. 10,000 connections ≈ 10 GB and a scheduler having a nervous breakdown — and that was one node's ceiling. Scaling horizontally meant on the order of a hundred more boxes to reach a million sockets, each one mostly paying to hold idle connections open.`,`We spent the weekend cutting the edge over to NGINX: four workers per box, one pinned per core, epoll doing readiness detection. The same hardware that died around 10k connections held 250k with headroom — and the CPUs finally tracked the actual traffic. Nothing about the kernel changed that weekend. The architecture did.`],problem:`How do you hold a million open sockets on commodity hardware without a million threads — and without the kernel's defaults quietly capping you at a thousand?`},tech:{stack:[`NGINX`,`epoll / kqueue`,`Event Loop`,`Worker Processes`,`TLS 1.3`,`sysctl Tuning`],note:`NGINX replaces a thread per connection with a handful of single-threaded workers running epoll-driven event loops. The kernel's readiness APIs and sysctl tuning are what let those loops hold a million sockets.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why does thread-per-connection die at 10k?`,body:[`Let's do the arithmetic out loud, because this is the whole lesson. Apache prefork: one process per connection, call it 10 MB resident. Ten thousand connections — one hundred gigabytes. The worker MPM trades processes for threads: about 1 MB of stack each, so 10k connections is 10 GB, plus a kernel scheduler juggling thousands of mostly-sleeping threads. Either way, memory and CPU burn linearly with concurrency, not with actual work.`,`Here's the part that took me years to internalize: a web connection is idle the overwhelming majority of its life. It's waiting on a slow human at the other end. Assigning a thread to it means paying the full capacity cost — stack, scheduler entry, context switches — for capacity you're never using. And the old readiness APIs, select and poll, made it worse: an O(N) scan per call, so even noticing which of 10k sockets is ready hurts.`,`So when someone says 'just add boxes' — sure, that works, but you're buying hardware proportional to concurrent connections, not to throughput. The fix has to change the cost per connection, not distribute it.`],quote:`The C10K problem isn't a hardware limit. It's an architectural one.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`RAM per conn`,`10k conns`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Prefork (process/conn)`,`~10 MB`,`~100 GB`,`Dead on arrival`],[`Worker MPM (thread/conn)`,`~1 MB`,`~10 GB`,`Hits the wall`],[`Event loop (NGINX)`,`a few KB`,`<1 GB`,`Scales to 1M`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — NGINX — 1M concurrent connections`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-nginx.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: one thread per core, epoll for everything else`,body:[`NGINX's answer is almost insultingly simple. One master process that owns configuration and worker lifecycle, and N worker processes — one per CPU core — each of them single-threaded. Each worker runs an event loop: call epoll_wait, get back only the sockets that are ready to do something, do exactly that much work, loop. Read what's available, write what the socket will accept, never block.`,`The kernel feature that makes this work is epoll (kqueue on BSD). select and poll hand you back an O(N) scan every call; epoll keeps a readiness structure in the kernel and returns only ready FDs — effectively O(1) whether you're watching a thousand sockets or a million. That's the difference between 'the loop slows down as connections grow' and 'it doesn't.'`,`Why not more threads, or an async runtime? Because one loop per core means no locks on the hot path, no preemption mid-request, and cache-friendly locality. The concurrency lives in the kernel's socket buffers; the program just reacts to readiness.`],quote:`Schedule on readiness, not on allocation — never pay for a thread that's only waiting.`,table:{headers:[`API`,`Cost per call`,`1M FDs`,`Era`],rows:[[`select()`,`O(N) scan, FD_SETSIZE 1024 cap`,`Impossible`,`1980s`],[`poll()`,`O(N) scan, no 1024 cap`,`Painful`,`1990s`],[`epoll / kqueue`,`~O(1), returns ready set only`,`Trivial`,`The C10M enabler`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — NGINX — 1M concurrent connections`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-nginx.html`}},{heading:`03 · Tuning for 1M: the kernel's defaults are a trap`,body:[`Architecture gets you the shape; tuning gets you the number. First ceiling: file descriptors. The OS default is 1,024 per process — you'll exhaust that at a thousand sockets per worker no matter how good your event loop is. Set worker_rlimit_nofile 1000000 in NGINX, fs.file-max system-wide, and limits.conf for the nginx user.`,`Second: worker_connections, which is per worker. Total capacity is roughly workers × worker_connections, so 16 cores × 65k ≈ a million. Third — and this one bites everyone running NGINX as a proxy — outbound connections eat ephemeral ports. The default range is ~28k ports, and closed sockets sit in TIME_WAIT for ~60 seconds, which caps you around 470 new upstream connections per second. Widen ip_local_port_range to 1024–65535, set tcp_tw_reuse=1, and keep connections alive to upstreams so you're not burning a port per request.`],quote:`At scale, the OS defaults are a self-inflicted denial of service.`,table:{headers:[`Knob`,`Default`,`Tuned`,`Why`],rows:[[`ulimit / worker_rlimit_nofile`,`1,024`,`1,000,000`,`FDs cap sockets per worker`],[`worker_connections`,`512`,`50,000–65,000`,`Per-worker socket capacity`],[`ip_local_port_range`,`~28k ports`,`1024–65535`,`Outbound proxy connections`],[`tcp_tw_reuse`,`0`,`1`,`Recycle TIME_WAIT sockets`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — NGINX — 1M concurrent connections`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-nginx.html`}},{heading:`04 · Where the CPU actually goes: TLS handshakes`,body:[`Here's the twist people miss: a million idle HTTPS connections is the easy case. An established TLS session costs you almost nothing per second — the event loop barely notices it. What melts CPUs is handshakes: asymmetric crypto per new connection. A million clients actively reconnecting is a very different machine than a million clients holding sockets open.`,`So you attack the per-handshake cost. TLS 1.3 collapses the handshake to one round trip. Session resumption tickets let returning clients skip the expensive part entirely. ECDSA certificates sign in roughly a tenth the time of RSA-2048. OCSP stapling takes an outbound fetch off the critical path. Idle is free; handshakes are the bill — size your cores for your reconnect rate, not your connection count.`],quote:`Idle connections are free. Handshakes are the bill.`,table:{headers:[`Mitigation`,`What it saves`,`Trade-off`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`TLS 1.3`,`1 RTT per handshake`,`Needs modern clients`,`Default on`],[`Session tickets`,`Full crypto on reconnect`,`Ticket key hygiene`,`Always`],[`ECDSA certs`,`~10x cheaper signing vs RSA-2048`,`Legacy client support`,`Use it`],[`OCSP stapling`,`OCSP fetch off hot path`,`Staple freshness window`,`Set and forget`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — NGINX — 1M concurrent connections`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-nginx.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`nginx-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`NGINX topology: master, workers, and the kernel readiness layer`,caption:`One master, one single-threaded worker per core, and epoll in the kernel deciding who gets woken up.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    C["~1M client sockets"] --> K["Linux kernel<br/>epoll readiness sets"]
    K -->|"epoll_wait(): only ready FDs"| W1["Worker 1<br/>single-threaded event loop"]
    K -->|"epoll_wait()"| W2["Worker 2<br/>single-threaded event loop"]
    K -->|"epoll_wait()"| WN["Worker N<br/>one per CPU core"]
    M["Master process<br/>config + worker lifecycle"] -.->|"spawns / reloads"| W1
    M -.-> W2
    M -.-> WN
    W1 --> U["Upstream apps / static files"]
    W2 --> U
    WN --> U`},{id:`event-loop-tick`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One tick of the event loop`,caption:`The worker never waits on a socket — it reacts to readiness and immediately returns to epoll_wait.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant K as Kernel epoll
    participant W as Worker event loop
    participant S as Client socket
    participant U as Upstream
    W->>K: epoll_wait()
    K-->>W: fd 42 readable, fd 91 writable
    W->>S: read(fd 42) - request bytes
    S-->>W: partial data, never block
    W->>U: proxy_pass on keepalive conn
    U-->>W: response bytes
    W->>S: write(fd 42) what the socket accepts
    Note over W,K: back to epoll_wait in microseconds<br/>the other ~100k sockets were never polled`}],takeaways:[`Memory rule of thumb: thread-per-connection ≈ 1 MB/conn (10 GB at 10k sockets); NGINX event loop ≈ a few KB/conn (<1 GB at 100k) — three orders of magnitude cheaper per idle connection.`,`Capacity formula: max connections ≈ workers × worker_connections. One worker per core with worker_connections 65536 → 16 cores ≈ 1M sockets.`,`Raise FD ceilings first: default 1,024 per process → worker_rlimit_nofile 1000000 and fs.file-max system-wide; past ~1k sockets per worker, architecture is irrelevant until this is fixed.`,`Proxy port budget: default ~28k ephemeral ports with 60s TIME_WAIT caps outbound at ~470 new connections/sec — set ip_local_port_range 1024–65535, tcp_tw_reuse=1, and keepalive to upstreams.`,`TLS sizing: handshakes, not idle connections, burn CPU — TLS 1.3 + session tickets + ECDSA certs (signing ~10x cheaper than RSA-2048) turn a million reconnecting clients from impossible into a core-count exercise.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — NGINX — 1M concurrent connections`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-nginx.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`mj--Sq7QnUb4IE`,note:`YARP vs Nginx Performance Showdown (Surprising Results!) covers this from a different angle.`}],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-webhooks`,pathId:`foundations-of-the-web`,order:5,title:`Webhooks`,subtitle:`A webhook is an HTTP POST you didn't ask for — sent by a third party when something interesting happens to your account on their system. Stripe, GitHub, Twilio, Shopify all ship th`,minutes:10,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`HMAC signing`,`HTTP POST`,`retry queue`],story:{headline:`The night 61 customers got two boxes each.`,paragraphs:[`14:00 during a blue-green cutover, the Saturday of Black Friday weekend. The on-call phone lights up: 214 orders in the last hour, 61 of them duplicated — same customer, same address, two shipping labels each. The fulfillment queue is cheerfully chewing through events Stripe already delivered once. Our webhook handler had been 'fine' for eight months.`,`The post-mortem found three sins stacked on top of each other. The handler parsed the JSON body before verifying the signature — and one legacy route never verified at all. It did full fulfillment synchronously, shipping label to CRM write, taking 35–50 seconds, well past Stripe's timeout. And the dedupe was a check-then-insert with no atomicity, so two racing retries both passed the check. Every timeout became a retry; every retry became a second shipment.`,`Worse: while we slept, someone found the unsigned legacy route and POSTed fake payment_intent.succeeded events at it. Free goods, real shipping. The fix wasn't clever — it was the boring contract: verify raw bytes first, ack in milliseconds, dedupe atomically, work in the background.`],problem:`How do you accept unauthenticated inbound HTTP from the internet, prove it's genuine, acknowledge it in seconds, and still make the side effects happen exactly once?`},tech:{stack:[`HTTP POST`,`HMAC-SHA256`,`Constant-Time Compare`,`Redis SETNX`,`Background Queue`,`Dead-Letter Queue`],note:`The receive path's moving parts: a signed POST arrives, gets constant-time HMAC verification and a timestamp check, an atomic Redis dedupe, a fast 200, and the real work happens in a background queue with a dead-letter tail.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just poll?`,body:[`Let's start with the obvious alternative, because someone watching is thinking it: just poll. Your integration wants to know when a payment intent succeeds, so hit GET /payment_intents/{id} every few seconds. What does that cost? You're burning the provider's rate limit on information that's usually 'no, nothing changed,' your latency floor is your poll interval, and at Black Friday scale you're re-polling thousands of intents that finished hours ago. Push flips the economics: one request per event that actually happened, latency down to a network round-trip.`,`But here's the part people miss — the trust model inverts. In a normal API call, you prove who you are with an API key; the server authenticates the client. A webhook is the mirror image: the provider has to prove who it is to you, because you are now a server accepting unauthenticated POSTs from the open internet. That single inversion is why signing, timestamps, and verification order exist at all.`],quote:`A webhook is a request you accept from someone you don't trust, that you have to be sure came from someone you do.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Polling`,`Simple, stateless, you control cadence`,`Stale by one interval, burns rate limit, mostly wasted calls`,`Low volume, no provider webhooks`],[`Webhooks (push)`,`Near-real-time, one request per real event`,`Must verify, dedupe, ack fast; endpoint is attack surface`,`Event-driven integrations at any scale`],[`Hybrid (push + reconcile)`,`Webhooks for speed, API polling as safety net`,`Two code paths to maintain`,`Money-moving, compliance-sensitive flows`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Webhooks`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-webhooks.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: sign first, parse never`,body:[`Here's the mechanism, and it's beautifully cheap. At signup the provider shares a secret with you. On every event they compute HMAC-SHA256 over the timestamp plus the raw body and ship it in a header. On receipt you recompute the same HMAC and compare: match, proceed; mismatch, 401 and log it. The whole thing costs microseconds of CPU.`,`The trap is ordering. The signature covers the canonical raw bytes — the exact bytes on the wire. If you parse the JSON into an object and re-serialize it, you've manufactured different bytes, and worse, you've already run parser-level attacks on untrusted input before checking anything. So: read the raw body, verify, then parse. And compare with a constant-time function — your language's FixedTimeEquals or equivalent — because naive string equality leaks timing information byte by byte.`,`Then the timestamp. It's inside the signed payload, so an attacker can't tamper with it, and it's your replay defense: reject anything older than about five minutes. An attacker who captures a POST in flight can't replay it next week, because you'll read the timestamp and drop it. That assumes your clock and the provider's agree within tolerance — NTP on your webhook hosts is now part of your security posture.`],quote:`The bytes you verify must be the bytes you parse — anything else is a different request.`,table:{headers:[`Defense`,`Stops`,`Cost`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`HMAC over raw body`,`Forged events, tampered payloads`,`Microseconds + one shared secret`,`Non-negotiable, do it first`],[`Timestamp tolerance (~5 min)`,`Replay of captured requests`,`One clock check + NTP discipline`,`Second-cheapest win after signing`],[`Constant-time compare`,`Timing side channels on the secret`,`One stdlib call`,`Free — always use it`],[`IP allowlisting`,`Casual scanners`,`Fragile, provider IPs change`,`Defense in depth only, never primary`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Webhooks`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-webhooks.html`}},{heading:`03 · At-least-once means duplicates are a feature`,body:[`Say it with me: every serious provider promises at-least-once delivery. Not exactly-once — nobody on the internet delivers exactly-once, because that would require the provider to know your side effects, and it can't. The contract is: you will see every event at least once, possibly more, and duplicates are the system working as designed, not a bug to file.`,`The standard implementation is embarrassingly simple. Every event carries a unique ID. On receipt, SETNX that ID in Redis with a TTL longer than the provider's retry window — Stripe retries for up to three days in live mode, so a seven-day TTL covers everyone. If SETNX wins, you're first: do the work. If it loses, the event was already handled: return 200 and skip. Atomic, because check-then-insert across two calls is exactly the race that shipped duplicate boxes at 11:45 when batch reconciliation started.`,`Ordering is the second lie. Stripe will sometimes deliver charge.refunded before charge.captured — retries, parallel delivery, network shuffling. Never reconstruct state from arrival order. For anything ordering-sensitive, treat the event as a hint and fetch current state from the provider's API. The API is the truth; the webhook is the doorbell.`],quote:`Design for every event arriving twice, and you'll be pleasantly surprised when it arrives once.`,table:{headers:[`Guarantee`,`What it really means`,`Your obligation`,`Reality check`],rows:[[`At-most-once`,`Fire and forget, losses are final`,`Accept silent data loss`,`Almost no provider offers this`],[`At-least-once`,`Duplicates guaranteed, no silent loss`,`Idempotent handlers + event-ID dedupe`,`The industry contract: Stripe, GitHub, Twilio`],[`Exactly-once`,`Requires provider knowledge of your side effects`,`Effectively your job, not theirs`,`Marketing term; build it from at-least-once + idempotency`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Webhooks`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-webhooks.html`}},{heading:`04 · The 10-second budget: ack fast, work later`,body:[`Here's the number that shapes the whole handler: providers time you out fast. GitHub gives you about 10 seconds, Twilio 15, Stripe roughly 30. If you're still working when the timer fires, the provider marks the delivery failed and schedules a retry — and now your slow handler is about to receive the same event again while it's still processing the first one. That's the retry storm from the story.`,`So the handler's contract is three moves: verify the signature, persist the event to a queue or table, return 200. Milliseconds, not seconds. The actual work — fulfillment, emails, database writes — happens in a background worker with its own retry policy and its own dead-letter queue. The webhook thread's only job is to say 'got it' convincingly.`,`The subtle win: once ack-fast is in place, your failure modes decouple. If the worker dies, the provider isn't retrying into a broken endpoint — the job sits in your queue where you can see it, retry it, or dead-letter it. You've converted 'provider thinks we're down' into 'we own the failure and can inspect it.'`],quote:`A webhook handler's entire job is to say 'got it' — the work belongs to someone else.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Time to 200`,`Duplicate risk`,`Failure visibility`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Sync processing inline`,`35–50s in our story`,`Every timeout = retry = duplicate`,`None — provider just sees failure`,`Never, at any scale`],[`Verify + enqueue + ack`,`Sub-second`,`Only provider-side retries`,`Full — queue and DLQ inspectable`,`The default choice`],[`Verify + write table + ack`,`Sub-second`,`Same, plus DB doubles as audit log`,`Full and queryable`,`When you need event history`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Webhooks`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-webhooks.html`}},{heading:`05 · When the pipe breaks: dead letters and reconciliation`,body:[`Last uncomfortable truth: webhooks are best-effort. If your endpoint is unhealthy, the provider retries with exponential backoff for hours or days — Stripe keeps at it for up to three days — and then gives up. Stripe sends you an email; GitHub shows recent deliveries in its dashboard; some providers tell no one. After the window closes, those events are gone from the pipe forever.`,`So you build the safety net yourself. Surface failed deliveries on a dashboard, alert on dead-lettered jobs, and — the part that actually saves you — run a daily reconciliation job that pulls current state from the provider's API and diffs it against yours. Missed a payout event at 3 AM during a deploy? Reconciliation finds it by morning and nobody gets paged.`,`This is the mental model to leave with: the webhook is the doorbell, the API is the house. The doorbell is fast and convenient, but you don't run a bank on the assumption that every visitor rings. Webhooks for speed, API for truth, reconciliation for honesty.`],quote:`Webhooks tell you something happened; the API tells you what's true.`,table:{headers:[`Failure mode`,`Symptom`,`Detection`,`Mitigation`],rows:[[`Endpoint down during retry window`,`Events silently lost after provider gives up`,`Provider delivery logs / dashboard`,`Daily API reconciliation`],[`Slow handler`,`Retry storms, duplicate side effects`,`Duplicate rate in dedupe store`,`Ack fast, move work to worker`],[`Worker crash after ack`,`Event acked but never processed`,`Queue depth / job age alerts`,`Worker retries + dead-letter queue`],[`Clock drift`,`Valid events rejected as stale`,`401 rate on fresh events`,`NTP on webhook hosts`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Webhooks`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-webhooks.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`webhook-receive-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The receive path: verify, dedupe, ack, then work`,caption:`Every decision on the hot path is cheap; every expensive step lives behind the queue.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    P["Provider<br/>Stripe / GitHub / Twilio"] -->|"POST + signature header"| EP["Webhook Endpoint"]
    EP --> V{"HMAC valid and<br/>timestamp fresh?"}
    V -->|"no"| R["401 reject + log"]
    V -->|"yes"| D{"SETNX event id<br/>in Redis, TTL 7d"}
    D -->|"already set"| A["200 OK - skip work"]
    D -->|"first time"| Q["Enqueue job"]
    Q --> A
    Q --> W["Background Worker"]
    W --> S{"Processed?"}
    S -->|"yes"| OK["Mark done"]
    S -->|"retries exhausted"| DLQ["Dead-letter queue"]
    DLQ --> REC["Daily API reconciliation"]`},{id:`duplicate-anatomy`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of a duplicate: retry meets idempotency`,caption:`The second delivery costs one Redis lookup and a 200 — no side effects fire twice.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant P as Provider
    participant E as Endpoint
    participant R as Redis
    participant Q as Queue
    participant W as Worker
    P->>E: POST evt_123, signed
    E->>E: Verify HMAC over raw body
    E->>R: SETNX evt:evt_123, TTL 7d
    R-->>E: OK, first delivery
    E->>Q: Enqueue fulfillment
    E-->>P: 200 OK in 200ms
    Note over P,E: Provider timeout is 10-30s, ack beats work
    Q->>W: Deliver job
    W->>W: Process and mark done
    P->>E: POST evt_123 again, retry
    E->>R: SETNX evt:evt_123
    R-->>E: nil, already set
    E-->>P: 200 OK, deduped, no work`}],takeaways:[`Verify HMAC-SHA256 over the raw request bytes before any parsing; compare with a constant-time function and reject events with timestamps older than 5 minutes.`,`Return 200 in under 10 seconds — validate, dedupe, enqueue, ack. Provider timeouts run 10–30s (GitHub 10s, Twilio 15s, Stripe ~30s) and every timeout converts into a guaranteed retry.`,`Dedupe on event ID with SETNX and a TTL of at least 7 days; Stripe retries up to 3 days, so your dedupe window must outlive every provider's retry schedule.`,`Never reconstruct state from event arrival order — for ordering-sensitive flows, re-fetch current state from the provider's API after the webhook arrives.`,`Assume some events arrive twice and some never arrive: run a daily reconciliation diff against the provider's API and alert on dead-lettered jobs within 15 minutes.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Webhooks`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-webhooks.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`mj-nbDYwxIeivs`,note:`Building Webhooks in .NET: Adding PostgreSQL Database Storage (Webhooks series part 2) covers this from a different angle.`},{id:`mj-mxsF7wOin-U`,note:`Fixing a Huge Performance Problem With My Webhooks Implementation (Webhooks series part 3) covers this from a different angle.`}],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`},{id:`fundamental-troubleshooting`,pathId:`foundations-of-the-web`,order:6,title:`Troubleshooting a website`,subtitle:`A user can't reach your website. Where's the problem? Use the request path as the troubleshooting tree: DNS, TCP, TLS, HTTP, app, database. Test each step; the first failure is the`,minutes:9,difficulty:3.5,tags:[`curl`,`dig`,`ping`,`top of funnel`],story:{headline:`3 AM: 'The site is down' — and everyone starts restarting things`,paragraphs:[`09:30 during a latency regression audit. Pager fires, Slack erupts, and within ninety seconds three engineers are doing three different things: one is rolling back the deploy from six hours ago, one is restarting nginx on the edge boxes, one is bouncing a database replica 'just in case'. Nobody has run a single test. Twenty minutes later the site is still down, the rollback is half-applied, and the VP is asking for an ETA nobody can give.`,`Meanwhile, one SRE who was awake anyway does something almost rude in its simplicity: curl -v https://shop.example.com. The output dies mid-handshake — 'certificate has expired'. The auto-renewal cron had been silently failing for two weeks, the cert died at midnight, and one command named the layer, the cause, and the fix: certbot renew, reload, done.`,`The rollback did nothing. The restarts did nothing. The actual fix took forty seconds — after a five-command walk down the request path. That's the whole lesson: the path from a browser to your database is a fixed sequence of layers, and the first one that says 'no' is your bug.`],problem:`Where exactly in the request path is the first failure — and how do you find it by testing instead of guessing?`},tech:{stack:[`dig`,`curl -v`,`nc`,`traceroute`,`openssl s_client`,`DevTools Network`],note:`One probe per layer of the request path — DNS, TCP, TLS, HTTP — plus the browser panel for when the page loads but lies. Each tool answers a single yes/no question, which is what makes bisection possible.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'restart everything' fails`,body:[`Here's the scene: pager fires, 'site is down', and within ninety seconds three people are doing three different things. One's rolling back the deploy, one's restarting nginx, one's bouncing a database replica. Nobody has run a single test. That's not troubleshooting — that's superstition with extra steps.`,`The discipline is boring and it works: the request path is DNS, then TCP, then TLS, then HTTP, then your app, then your database. Each layer either works or it doesn't. You test them top to bottom, and the first layer that says 'no' is where you dig. Every test that passes eliminates half the remaining suspects — bisection, the same trick you'd use on a bad commit.`,`And here's the kicker: the first failure is usually the symptom, and the symptom usually is the bug. Cert expired at midnight? The 'site is down' report and the expired cert are the same event. You don't need a war room; you need one command per layer.`],quote:`Every blind restart is a variable you just added to a system you don't understand yet.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Guess & restart`,`Feels fast, zero learning curve`,`Adds variables, destroys evidence, often makes it worse`,`Never`],[`Bisect the request path`,`Each test halves the search space; evidence preserved`,`Requires knowing the probe for each layer`,`Always`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Troubleshooting a website`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-troubleshooting.html`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: DNS first`,body:[`Start at the top. dig +short yoursite.com @1.1.1.1 — you're asking a known-good public resolver what the world sees. If that returns the right IP, DNS is fine as far as the internet is concerned, and you just skipped the entire DNS rabbit hole in five seconds.`,`Now compare vantage points. If the public resolver is right but your local stub resolver returns garbage, the bug is in your network's DNS chain — VPN, corporate resolver, a /etc/hosts entry someone forgot about. If even the authoritative nameservers answer wrong, the bug is at your DNS host: expired zone, botched migration, deleted record.`,`Three dig commands and you've cleanly split 'the internet can't find us' from 'our building can't find us' from 'nobody can find us'. That's the whole game: compare answers between vantage points until only one explanation survives.`],quote:`Half of 'the site is down' is actually 'the name is down' — check DNS before you touch a single server.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Troubleshooting a website`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-troubleshooting.html`}},{heading:`03 · TCP: is anyone home?`,body:[`DNS gave you an IP. Now nc -vz that-ip 443. Two failure modes, two completely different bugs. 'Connection refused' means your packets arrived and something actively said no — the host is up, but nothing is listening on that port. Check the listener, the load balancer target pool, the security group.`,`'Timeout' is the opposite: your packets went into the void. That's routing or firewall, and traceroute shows you the last hop that answered — the black hole starts right after it. And watch out for asymmetric routing: your SYN gets there fine, but the reply takes a different path home and dies. The server logs will even show the connection attempt arriving. Cruel, but very real.`,`The payoff of this layer is negative space: if nc connects, you never spend another second tonight thinking about networks. Cross it off and move down.`],quote:`'Refused' means someone's home and ignoring you; 'timeout' means nobody's home. Different diseases, different doctors.`,table:{headers:[`nc result`,`Meaning`,`Next probe`],rows:[[`Connection refused`,`Host reachable, nothing listening on the port`,`Check LB targets, service status, security group`],[`Timeout`,`Packets dropped in transit`,`traceroute, firewall rules, asymmetric routing`],[`Connects`,`TCP layer healthy`,`Move down to TLS`]]},source:{label:`sharpoverride — Troubleshooting a website`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-troubleshooting.html`}},{heading:`04 · TLS and HTTP: where the 3 AM story actually ended`,body:[`TCP is open, so the network folks are off the hook. Next: openssl s_client -connect host:443 -servername host. You'll see the full handshake, the certificate chain, the expiry dates — and in our story, 'certificate has expired'. The renewal cron had been failing silently for two weeks; the cert died at midnight and took the site with it.`,`If the handshake is clean, curl -v is your stethoscope. One command covers four layers — DNS lookup, TCP connect, TLS handshake, then the actual HTTP exchange — and prints the response code and headers at the end. A 502 points at the app or its upstreams; a 200 with a broken page sends you to the browser DevTools network panel to find which subrequest is red.`,`Notice the pattern: each tool names its layer in its own output. Once the output tells you the layer, you're not debugging anymore — you're just reading the answer.`],quote:`curl -v is a stethoscope: one command, four layers, and it tells you which one has the murmur.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Troubleshooting a website`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-troubleshooting.html`}},{heading:`05 · The bottom of the funnel: app and database`,body:[`If DNS, TCP, TLS, and HTTP all pass, congratulations — the infrastructure is innocent, and the bug lives in your code or its dependencies. The funnel narrows fast now: error rates, logs, connection pool saturation, that migration someone ran at 5 PM.`,`This is where bisection pays off hardest, because you arrive with certainty about everything below. You're not wondering if it's the network while you read stack traces. The first layer that said 'no' told you where to stand, and everything above it is exonerated.`,`And when you find it — the expired cert, the full disk, the exhausted connection pool — notice it's almost always exactly what the first failing symptom pointed at. The symptom is the bug wearing a costume.`],quote:`By the time you reach the app layer, everything below it is innocent — that certainty is the whole reward for walking the path.`,source:{label:`sharpoverride — Troubleshooting a website`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-troubleshooting.html`}}],diagrams:[{id:`bisection-tree`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The request-path bisection tree`,caption:`Top-to-bottom decision tree: one probe per layer, and the first 'no' branch is where the bug lives.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Site down"] --> B{"dig +short site.com @1.1.1.1"}
    B -->|wrong or no answer| B1["DNS layer — compare local stub vs authoritative NS"]
    B -->|right IP| C{"nc -vz IP 443"}
    C -->|timeout| C1["Network layer — traceroute, firewall, asymmetric routing"]
    C -->|refused| C2["Listener layer — LB target, dead service, security group"]
    C -->|connects| D{"openssl s_client -connect IP:443"}
    D -->|handshake fails| D1["TLS layer — expired cert, broken chain, SNI mismatch"]
    D -->|handshake OK| E{"curl -v https://site.com"}
    E -->|5xx or timeout| E1["App and upstream layer — logs, error rates, connection pool"]
    E -->|200 but page broken| E2["Frontend layer — DevTools network panel, failing subrequests"]`},{id:`probe-session`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One probe per layer: the 3 AM session that worked`,caption:`The disciplined version of the incident: each probe exonerates a layer until the expired cert names itself.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant You as On-call engineer
    participant Net as Resolver and network
    participant Edge as Load balancer 443
    participant App as App and database

    You->>Net: dig +short site.com @1.1.1.1
    Net-->>You: 203.0.113.10 — DNS is innocent
    You->>Edge: nc -vz 203.0.113.10 443
    Edge-->>You: connects — TCP is innocent
    You->>Edge: openssl s_client -servername site.com
    Edge-->>You: certificate has expired — FIRST FAILURE
    Note over You,Edge: Stop bisecting. Start fixing.
    You->>Edge: certbot renew, then reload nginx
    You->>App: curl -v https://site.com
    App-->>You: 200 OK — site restored`}],takeaways:[`Walk the path in order — DNS, TCP, TLS, HTTP, app, DB — and fix nothing until a test names the first failing layer; five commands usually suffice.`,`Run dig +short yoursite.com @1.1.1.1 first: a correct answer exonerates all of DNS in under 10 seconds.`,`Read nc's two failures differently: 'refused' means the host is up with nothing listening; 'timeout' means packets are dying — traceroute the hop where replies stop.`,`Run curl -v before any restart: one command covers four layers and prints the failing one — a 30-second test that prevents an hour of flailing.`,`Cap blind restarts at zero before diagnosis: every untested restart adds a variable and destroys the evidence you'll need.`],sources:[{kind:`website`,label:`sharpoverride — Troubleshooting a website`,url:`https://sharpoverride.github.io/topics/fundamental-troubleshooting.html`,author:`sharpoverride`,note:`The written lesson. Same voice, same diagrams, more room to breathe.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`,kind:`topic`}];export{e as lessons};